You can't — Vite maintainers declined the callback approach for now. As bluwy explains, "We don't want to accept a function for now as it's difficult to merge if other plugins were to add more conditions" [4].

Instead, import Vite's default list and spread it yourself: use `defaultClientConditions` for client builds or `defaultServerConditions` for server builds [4]. `defaultClientConditions` is documented at https://vite.dev/config/shared-options#resolve-conditions [4].

```js
import { defaultClientConditions } from 'vite'

export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    conditions: ['workspace', ...defaultClientConditions],
  },
})
```

The Node.js CLI alignment argument also doesn't apply yet, since it depends on Node support (nodejs/node#57688) [4].

The excerpts don't give a confirmed root cause for the v8 regression itself — the thread is still open as a bug [1][2]. What they do show is that the failure has two distinct symptoms: in `vite build` the `?inline` import fails with `[UNLOADABLE_DEPENDENCY] Could not load ... No such file or directory (os error 2)` [5], while in development (`vite serve`) the request is rejected with a 403 because the path is "outside of Vite serving allow list" [1]. For the dev case, a maintainer suggests excluding `*.der` from `server.fs.deny`, since it's in the default list [3]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  server: {
    fs: {
      deny: ['!**/*.der'] // adjust to exclude *.der from the default deny list
    }
  }
}
```

The excerpts do not state why v8 changed this behavior, so I can't confirm the underlying cause.

There is currently no supported way to do this: the framework "has no supported way to suppress, replace, or customise the error emitted by ModuleRunner’s automatic full-reload path" [5]. The logging comes from the runner's HMR/full-reload re-import path, not from the explicit `runner.import()` call [2][3], so catching the initial import rejection doesn't help.

The issue proposes three possible APIs (none implemented yet) [5]:

```js
// Option A
{ runner: { logReloadErrors: false } }

// Option B
await server.environments.ssr.runner.import(id, { logErrors: false });

// Option C
{ runner: { onReloadError(error, context) { return true; } } }
```

With the callback form, returning `true` would mean the framework handled the error, so Vite would skip its default terminal output while the reload still fails and the error is still exposed [4]. The default behaviour would remain unchanged [4].

The failure is an upstream rolldown issue: a member reference like `Context.Provider` is emitted without the variable being declared or imported, so the production bundle throws `Uncaught ReferenceError: Context is not defined` [3]. A maintainer attributes it to rolldown issue #9964 [1]. It appears with rolldown 1.1.0–1.1.3 and goes away when overriding `"vite>rolldown": 1.0.3` [2]. The workaround is to disable lazy barrel optimization:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  build: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      experimental: { lazyBarrel: false }
    }
  }
}
```

This same setting also fixed a similar `__exportAll is not defined` error [5].

With `experimental.bundledDev` enabled in Vite 8.1.0, importing a locale from `date-fns/locale` throws a browser-side `ReferenceError: formatDistance$94 is not defined` (e.g. in `af.js`), while the same source works with bundled dev disabled [1][3][4]. The reporter notes that `af.js` shouldn't even be included since only `pl` is imported, suggesting it was "tree shaken halfway" [1]. The exact root cause isn't stated in these excerpts; a commenter links PR #22801 and offers a temporary workaround of disabling `lazyBarrel` in `rolldownOptions.experimental` [2]:

```js
rolldownOptions: {
  experimental: {
    lazyBarrel: command !== 'serve'
  },
}
```

The failure is a Rolldown CJS namespace interop issue, not something specific to `backbone-relational`: a CJS namespace import snapshots the current `module.exports` properties before a later CJS side-effect import mutates the same export object [1]. That's why `import * as Backbone from 'backbone'` followed by `import 'backbone-relational'` works in Vite dev but fails in the Vite 8 production build, throwing `can't access property "extend", Mo.RelationalModel is undefined` [1][4]. Default and named imports still work because they keep/read the original CJS export object [1]. The durable fix is expected in Rolldown first [1].

As a workaround, use a default import instead of a namespace import [3]:

```js
import Backbone from "backbone";
import "backbone-relational";

Backbone.RelationalModel.extend({});
```

There is no built-in Vite option for this yet; the feature request for a `maxWarnings: number` config option is still pending triage [1]. The maintainers currently prefer a plugin-based approach [5]. You can use a plugin that counts warnings via `onLog` and throws in `buildEnd` [2][3]:

```ts
function failOnWarningsPlugin(): Rolldown.Plugin {
  let warningCount = 0;
  return {
    name: 'fail-on-warnings',
    onLog(level) {
      if (level === 'warn') warningCount++;
    },
    buildEnd() {
      if (!warningCount) return;
      throw new Error(`${warningCount} warnings present`);
    },
  };
}
```

Note that throwing will log an error stack trace [4], and `process.exit` is discouraged due to Node.js behaviors like not flushing stdio buffers [4].

The breakage is an upstream esbuild issue (evanw/esbuild#4436) triggered by the esbuild version resolved in Vite 7.3.2, not by project-specific code [3][5]. It's tracked as a bug: upstream / plugin: legacy issue, and the suggested workaround is to tell esbuild that destructuring is supported [1][4]:

```ts
// vite.config.js
export default {
  esbuild: {
    supported: {
      destructuring: true
    }
  }
}
```

Alternatively, pinning esbuild to 0.27.4 via `overrides` makes the legacy build succeed again [5]. A maintainer notes this won't be a problem in v8 because the legacy-modern browser threshold was bumped, and suggests possibly setting `supported.destructuring: true` in v7 while telling users to use v8 for the fix [2]. The excerpts don't include the exact error stack or the specific esbuild change that causes it.

Vite 8's dependency optimization reorders ECharts' internal methods: in the optimized output, the `install` method runs before `init_install` has executed, causing `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'type')`, whereas in Vite 5 the `init_install` method in `node_modules/echarts/lib/component/dataset/install` executes before `install` [1]. The issue is labeled "bug: upstream" and reproduces with `vite@8.0.3` (also with `rolldown@1.0.0-rc.13`), while `vite@5.4.11` behaves normally [1][5]. A workaround for dev is setting `optimizeDeps.rolldownOptions.output.strictExecutionOrder: true` [2]:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  optimizeDeps: {
    rolldownOptions: {
      output: {
        strictExecutionOrder: true
      }
    }
  },
});
```

The excerpts don't explain the underlying cause of the reordering beyond it being an upstream issue.

Enable it explicitly in your Vite config — it is not on by default:

```ts
export default defineConfig({
  resolve: {
    tsconfigPaths: true,
  },
});
```

That is the documented usage [5], and the docs describe it as something you enable manually [2]. Note that the native option only accepts a boolean and selects tsconfigs internally via unconfigurable `includes` detection, so you cannot specify a target project [4]. If you need explicit project selection (e.g. `projects: ["tsconfig.json"]`), use `vite-tsconfig-paths` instead [3][5]. Also be aware of a known bug: with a symlinked shared tsconfig package, paths can misresolve to package fallback targets instead of source [1].

The excerpts don't contain a confirmed fix for this bug — it's an open issue [1][4]. What they do establish is the trigger and the workarounds that were verified to work:

- The failure only occurs when `tsconfig.json` has `references`, the `paths` live in a referenced config like `tsconfig.app.json`, and the importer is a virtual module whose ID contains a query string (e.g. `file.tsx?tsr-split=component`) [2][4].
- Moving all content from `tsconfig.app.json` into `tsconfig.json` (eliminating `references`) fixes it [2].
- Adding `paths` directly to the root `tsconfig.json` alongside `references` also works, because Vite finds paths in the root without delegating to a referenced config [2].
- Disabling TanStack Router's `autoCodeSplitting` also fixes it, since no virtual modules with query strings are generated [2].

So the practical workaround is to duplicate your `paths` in the root `tsconfig.json`:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": { "@components/*": ["src/components/*"] }
  },
  "references": [{ "path": "./tsconfig.app.json" }]
}
```

This is a TypeScript limitation, not a Vite bug: the async `defineConfig` callback's return type widens `minify` to `string`, which doesn't match the expected literal union, producing error 2769 [1][2]. The fix is to add `as const` [2]:

```ts
export default defineConfig(async () => ({
  plugins: [react()],
  build: {
    minify: 'terser' as const,
  },
}))
```

Vite 7.2.x removes `/*! ... */` comments because they're treated as "legal comments" by most tools, and esbuild's minification strips legal comments by default [1]. A maintainer notes that `/*! */` isn't actually a way to mark comments as "important" (despite what PurgeCSS docs suggest), so relying on that behavior isn't robust [1]. The suggested workaround is to set `esbuild.legalComments: 'inline'` [4]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  esbuild: {
    legalComments: 'inline',
  },
}
```

Alternatively, ensure PurgeCSS runs before esbuild minification (e.g., via postcss) [1].

There is currently no supported way to configure the lock file location — the dependency optimizer assumes a fixed location, and if the lock file isn't found the hash is computed from an empty string, so the cache never changes [1]. The requested `lockFilePath` configuration property is only a suggestion in the issue, not an existing option [1]. A maintainer noted Vite checks the internal lockfile representing `node_modules` state (e.g. `./node_modules/.pnpm/lock.yaml` for pnpm), which differs from the normal lockfile, and was unsure an exposed option could be properly documented [5]. As an alternative, they suggested adding an entry like this to the lockfile detection code [5]:

```js
{
  path: '.rush/temp/shrinkwrap-deps.json',
  // Included in lockfile
  checkPatchesDir: false,
  manager: 'pnpm',
},
```

Note that `getLockfileHash` searches upwards from the config root, so it should work as long as the config root is inside the project directory [3].

Vite doesn't treat `*.php` files specially, so it doesn't know about them and won't reload on their changes — this is expected behavior [2]. The regression in 7.1 is likely due to PR #20391 [5]. As a workaround, add a plugin that sends a full reload when a `.php` file changes [5]:

```js
plugins: [
	{
		name: 'reload-non-vite-code',
		hotUpdate(ctx) {
			if (ctx.file.endsWith(".php")) {
				ctx.server.environments.client.hot.send({
					type: 'full-reload',
				})
			}
		},
	}
]
```

Alternatively, `vite-plugin-full-reload` should work [2].

The type error occurs because the `importers` option is typed from the `sass` package rather than `sass-embedded` [2][3]. A workaround is to cast the importer to the `sass` type [3]:

```ts
import {defineConfig} from 'vite';
import {NodePackageImporter} from 'sass-embedded';
import {NodePackageImporter as DartSassPackageImporter} from 'sass';

export default defineConfig({
  css: {
    preprocessorOptions: {
      scss: {
        api: 'modern-compiler',
        importers: [
          new NodePackageImporter() as unknown as DartSassPackageImporter,
        ],
      },
    },
  },
});
```

A contributor also confirmed that reversing the order of checking sass options fixes it, using `IsAny<SassEmbeddedStringOptionsAsync> extends false ? SassEmbeddedStringOptionsAsync : DartSassStringOptionsAsync` [1]. Note that using `NodePackageImporter` from `sass` instead produces a runtime error: `importer.findFileUrl is not a function` [2].

The error occurs because Vite does not support processing CJS modules, and in dev mode the SSR-only module (e.g. `jsdom`) isn't being externalized, so it gets processed by Vite and hits the CJS `require` [1]. Dependencies are externalized by default, but Vite doesn't recognize a virtual id like `server-only:jsdom` as a dependency, so it isn't externalized [2]. In the reported case, `pnpm run build && pnpm run preview` works fine while `pnpm run dev` throws `ReferenceError: require is not defined` [3].

The suggested workaround is to make the virtual module a facade that re-exports the real dependency, so it goes through Vite's internal logic without involving plugins [1][2]:

```js
const p = {
  name: 'server-only-module',
  async resolveId(id, importer, {ssr}) {
    const prefix = 'server-only:';
    if (id.startsWith(prefix)) {
      if (ssr) return id
      return '\0empty-module';
    }
  },
  load(id) {
    if (id === '\0empty-module') return 'export {}';
    const prefix = 'server-only:';
    if (id.startsWith(prefix)) {
      return `export * from ${JSON.stringify(id.slice(prefix.length))}`
    }
  },
}
```

There's no built-in option yet: the request is to extend `build.modulePreload` (which currently only disables JS chunk preloading) to also allow disabling CSS preloading [4]. A maintainer notes Vite could add such an option, but per-import disabling may not be feasible since a CSS chunk can be shared between router-managed and non-managed dynamic imports [1].

As a workaround, a maintainer suggests removing the built-in `vite:build-import-analysis` plugin for build (not recommended for production, may break) [3]. A user reports that in Vite 8 this instead requires removing `native:import-analysis-build` [2]:

```js
{
  name: 'preload-remover',
  configResolved(config) {
    const pI = config.plugins.findIndex(
      (p) => p.name === 'native:import-analysis-build',
    );
    (config.plugins as Plugin[]).splice(pI, 1);
  },
}
```

For dev, it might be possible by swapping the `updateStyle` function implementation, though making that configurable isn't confirmed [3].

The hang is an upstream Rollup bug, not a Vite bug: it's tracked in rollup/rollup#5848 [1]. In one case it gets stuck while loading a CommonJS module (`fixPolyfills.js?commonjs-es-import`) in the commonjs plugin [4], and it appears related to the number of imports — with 20+ language files imported, the build hangs as soon as a dependency changes [2]. It started in v6 (did not happen with vite 5.4.14) [3][5].

Workaround: raise the parallel file limit [1][5]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  build: {
    rollupOptions: { maxParallelFileOps: 100 } // or a larger number
  }
}
```

Setting `build.commonjsOptions.strictRequires: 'auto'` also works but can make builds indeterministic in some cases, so `maxParallelFileOps` is recommended instead [1][4].

Sass's modern API passes the aliased path to Vite already stripped of the alias segment — e.g. `@/../node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions` arrives as `node_modules/bootstrap/scss/_functions` — so the relative resolution breaks [1]. This is a limitation of Sass's modern API, which is the default from Vite 6, and there's no easy way to solve it, so it was closed as won't-fix [1][2][3]. As a workaround you can switch to the legacy API [2]:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  css: {
    preprocessorOptions: {
      sass: { api: 'legacy' } // or scss: { api: 'legacy' }
    }
  }
}
```

The failure comes from Vite v5 matching Prisma's `browser` export instead of `node` for `.prisma/client` (Vite v6 matches the correct export) [1]. A workaround is to force the `/index` import in your `prisma:rebase` plugin [1]:

```js
return code.replaceAll(
  /require\((['"])\.prisma\/client\/default/g,
  "require($1../../.prisma/client/index"
);
```

Also move the Prisma plugins before the React Router plugin [1]. A cleaner approach is to generate the client as `@prisma/client-generated` via `output` in the schema, replace imports of `@prisma/client` with it, and use a single `prisma:build` plugin that defines `__dirname`/`__filename` as `import.meta.dirname`/`import.meta.filename` and replaces `eval("__dirname")` with `import.meta.dirname` [3]. At runtime you may still need to copy the Prisma binary (e.g. `libquery_engine-darwin-arm64.dylib.node`) into the `index.func` directory, outside `index.func/assets` [2].

To disable HMR, set `server.hmr: false` in your Vite config — a maintainer confirms this "should work" [2]. Note that the reporter still saw "failed to connect to WebSocket" errors even with HMR set to `false` [1], so if you also want to suppress the WebSocket entirely, the workaround discussed is to short-circuit `setupWebSocket` in the client code [3][4][5]:

```js
function setupWebSocket(protocol, hostAndPath, onCloseWithoutOpen) {
  if (!socketEnabled) return;
  // ...
}
```

That `setupWebSocket` change is only a temporary workaround (e.g. via Chrome local overrides) [3], not an official option.
